<onlyinclude>'''NPU architecture''' is best characterized by the mapping of neural-network computation and data movement onto specialized arithmetic units, on-chip storage, interconnect, and a compiler/runtime stack. The effective operating point depends on workload shape, precision, memory traffic, and utilization.</onlyinclude>

== Computational model ==
For a dense matrix product <math>C=AB</math> with <math>A\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times K}</math> and <math>B\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times N}</math>, the conventional algorithm performs <math>MNK</math> multiply–accumulate operations (MACs). If one MAC is counted as two arithmetic operations, its nominal work is <math>2MNK</math> operations; some hardware vendors use different counting conventions. This is an algorithmic count, not a prediction of runtime.

A simplified compute bound is <math>t_{\mathrm{compute}}\geq MNK/(P f u)</math>, where <math>P</math> is the number of MACs issued per cycle, <math>f</math> is frequency, and <math>0&lt;u\leq 1</math> is achieved utilization. Actual time is also constrained by data transfer, synchronization, unsupported operations, and software overhead. The expression assumes a steady workload with the stated utilization and does not account for startup or tiling overhead.

== Memory hierarchy and dataflow ==
NPU implementations may contain register files, scratchpad SRAM, shared on-chip buffers, DMA engines, and external memory interfaces. Weight-stationary, output-stationary, and row-stationary mappings describe which data are preferentially retained locally. They are architectural strategies rather than universal properties of every NPU.

Chen et al. demonstrated an Eyeriss spatial CNN accelerator with 168 processing elements and row-stationary dataflow, reporting 35 AlexNet convolution frames/s at 278 mW under the authors' specified evaluation conditions.<ref name="eyeriss">Chen, Y.-H., Krishna, T., Emer, J., and Sze, V. (2017). [https://doi.org/10.1109/JSSC.2016.2616357 Eyeriss: An Energy-Efficient Reconfigurable Accelerator for Deep Convolutional Neural Networks]. ''IEEE Journal of Solid-State Circuits'' 52(1), 127–138.</ref> Those measurements must not be extrapolated to arbitrary models, newer accelerators, or full application energy.

== Precision and numerical correctness ==
Inference accelerators frequently support quantized integer arithmetic; some support multiple integer or floating-point formats. Lower precision reduces storage and transfer demands but may require calibration, scale management, wider accumulation, and validation against task-level accuracy requirements. Support for an operation or data type is implementation-specific.

Arm documents Ethos-U85 configurations from 128 to 2048 MAC units, INT8 weights, INT8/INT16 activations, and support for CNN, RNN, and transformer networks.<ref name="arm">Arm. [https://support.arm.com/compute-ip/ethos-u85 Ethos-U85 Product Support], specifications and software components (accessed 2026-11-21).</ref> Arm's quoted peak of 256 GOP/s to 4 TOP/s at 1 GHz is a configuration-dependent theoretical rating, not measured throughput for all models.

== Compiler, runtime and system boundary ==
A model compiler or graph partitioner selects supported operators, transforms tensor layouts, allocates scratchpads, and schedules transfers. The runtime and host coordinate invocation and fallback execution. Unsupported operations can fragment a graph into multiple partitions and introduce synchronization and data-copy costs. Therefore, meaningful comparisons require the same model, input shape, precision/accuracy target, batch size, memory constraints, and measurement boundary.

== Worked quantitative check ==
For <math>M=N=K=256</math>, dense multiplication requires <math>256^3=16{,}777{,}216</math> MACs, or <math>33{,}554{,}432</math> arithmetic operations under the two-operations-per-MAC convention. A hypothetical 128-MAC/cycle device at 1 GHz and perfect utilization has a compute-only lower bound of <math>16{,}777{,}216/(128\times10^9)\approx 0.1311</math> ms. This excludes memory traffic, tiling, and launch overhead and is '''not''' a measured Ethos-U85 latency.

== Limitations and provenance ==
* The generic NPU label spans different architectures; no single dataflow, precision, or programming interface is universal.
* Vendor specifications document supported configurations and theoretical limits; peer-reviewed silicon measurements provide workload-specific evidence.
* End-to-end energy and latency cannot be inferred from arithmetic throughput alone.

